PHIS for Debian
================

Install layout
--------------

- Application root: `/usr/lib/phis`
- Standalone server root: `/usr/lib/phis/server`
- CLI: `/usr/bin/phis`, operating on `/srv/phis/stages/prod` unless `PHIS_ROOT`
  or `--root` names another stage
- Default stage root: `/srv/phis/stages/prod`
- Optional stage environment file: `/srv/phis/stages/<stage>/.env`

The packaged `systemd` template keeps the application working directory under
`/usr/lib/phis/server` and reads the mutable stage configuration through
`PHIS_ROOT`.
The default `prod` stage listens on `127.0.0.1:5301`.

The installed `/usr/bin/phis` runs the bundled wrapper under
`/usr/lib/phis/server/phis`, so the CLI and the standalone runtime use the
same traced dependency set. It sets `PHIS_ROOT` to `/srv/phis/stages/prod` when
the variable is unset; the wrapper alone would take `/usr/lib/phis/server`,
which holds no configuration.

`server` is a fixed name the packaging chooses. Next's standalone output nests the
application under the directory it was built from, and the packaging renames it on
the way in rather than repeating that name -- a checkout renamed on disk must not
be able to move an installed path.

Runtime baseline: Node `24.21.0` or later, for the runtime and the CLI alike.
Earlier releases hold on to rendered pages under load (a leak in React Flight
rendering fixed in Node 24.21).

No runtime configuration is stored under `/etc/phis`. Stage-local mutable state
belongs under `/srv/phis/stages/<stage>/`, while `phis-host` keeps its own host
configuration under `/var/lib/phis/phis-host/`.

Server Actions encryption key
-----------------------------

Next.js encrypts the arguments a Server Action is bound to, so that the browser
can neither read them nor choose them. The key for that is generated by
`next build` and travels inside the artifact, which means every installation of
the same `.deb` would otherwise hold the same one.

`postinst` therefore writes a fresh `NEXT_SERVER_ACTIONS_ENCRYPTION_KEY` into
`/srv/phis/stages/<stage>/.env` when that file does not already carry one, and
leaves it alone on upgrades. The runtime reads it from the environment and
prefers it over the key baked into the build, so no rebuild is involved.

Two cases need a hand:

- Several hosts serving one installation must share one value, otherwise a form
  submitted against one host cannot be decrypted by another.
- A stage moved to a new host should take its `.env` along rather than start with
  a new key while sessions are in flight.

The runtime refuses to start in production when the value is missing or is not
base64 of 32 bytes.

Web server templates
--------------------

`postinst` copies `/usr/share/phis/templates/nginx/phis.conf` to
`/etc/nginx/sites-available/phis.conf` and the Apache template to
`/etc/apache2/sites-available/phis.conf` when the directory exists and the file
is not there yet. Neither is enabled or reloaded; set `server_name`, add TLS,
link it and reload by hand.

Both proxy to a pool rather than to one address, because a pool is what the
keep-alive rule applies to. Node closes an idle keep-alive connection after 5
seconds; a proxy that holds its side longer hands out a connection the runtime
has already closed, and the request dies -- a `GET` is retried, a `POST` (a
login, a publish) answers `502`. nginx therefore gets `keepalive 64` with
`keepalive_timeout 4s`, Apache `ttl=4` on each `BalancerMember`.

The pool lists the instances this package runs: `phis@prod.service` on
`127.0.0.1:5301`, plus a commented line for a second instance of the same stage,
which needs a port of its own. Another stage is another installation with its own
database and belongs in its own `upstream` and `server` block.

`Host` is passed through unchanged (`$http_host`, `ProxyPreserveHost On`): on a
port other than 80 or 443 nginx's `$host` drops the port that the browser's
`Referer` keeps, and the form relay compares the two and refuses every submit.
The nginx template holds a `map` and an `upstream`, which nginx allows only in
the `http` context -- which is where Debian includes `sites-enabled/*`.

Database bootstrap
------------------

This package does not create PostgreSQL roles or databases automatically.

Recommended local naming for the default stage:

- database: `phis_prod`
- user: `phis_prod_user`

The package-created setup UI can write `config/phis-runtime.json` and apply the
application schema to an existing database connection, but it does not create
the PostgreSQL role or database itself.

If a local PostgreSQL instance is used, create the role and database before
completing the setup UI or use `phis-host` later for explicit stage/database
bootstrap.

Host integration
----------------

`phis-host` is not required for the packaged runtime to start, but it is the
recommended companion for host-side bootstrap and lifecycle tasks such as:

- stage initialization
- PostgreSQL bootstrap
- privileged service wiring
- nginx/apache installation workflows

Build workflow
--------------

Two steps, in this order:

1. Build the artifacts in the workspace:

       pnpm install
       pnpm --filter "@phis/server..." run build
       pnpm --filter @phis/server run build:cli

   The trailing `...` builds `@phis/contracts` first, which the server build
   resolves through `dist/`.

   This produces `.next/standalone`, `.next/static` and `dist/phis.mjs`.

2. Package them:

       ./scripts/build-deb.sh

   It copies the checkout -- including those three artifacts, without
   `.next/cache` and the workspace `node_modules` -- to
   `/tmp/phis-builds/phis/src` and builds there. The resulting `.deb`,
   `.changes` and `.buildinfo` land in `/tmp/phis-builds/phis/artifacts/`
   instead of the project parent directory.

The package build itself only copies. It runs no package manager, reads no
registry and needs no network: `debian/rules` checks that the three artifacts
are present and otherwise names the command that produces them. This is why the
build has no toolchain among its `Build-Depends` -- the build the artifacts come
out of belongs to the workspace, which carries the lockfile and `@phis/contracts`,
neither of which is part of this source package. The consequence to be aware of:
the `.deb` is not reproducible from the source package alone, and `debian/rules
clean` deliberately leaves `.next` and `dist` alone, because they are this
build's input rather than its output.
